From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:43:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/36] download: fail a post-message that is gone from the
 server
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A download-on-demand message whose post-message the server no longer has
spun forever instead of failing: no percentage, no failure, no retry
button. A chatmail relay expiring the message is enough to reach this.

The failure decision in `download_msgs()` is keyed on `available_post_msgs`,
but that flag never survives for a message the user can tap:
`download_known_post_messages_without_pre_message()` runs right before
`download_msgs()` on every cycle and drops every entry whose pre-message is
already in the database (`rfc724_mid_exists` matches the pre-message stub).
So every error took the "will be downloaded once it arrives" branch, kept
the queue row, and retried forever. When the relay had expunged the
message, the chunked path's up-front `uid_fetch_meta` probe bailed before
anything else ran, and the stale `imap` row sent the next cycle straight
back to it.

Proof that the message is gone now comes from the relay instead of the
flag. `forget_imap_location()` runs where the chunked path learns that a
relay does not serve the UID anymore — the up-front probe finds nothing or
finds it `\Deleted`, or a later chunk comes back empty and the re-probe
confirms it — and deletes that relay's row. If another relay still has a
location, the message stays queued and the inboxes are interrupted, so that
relay downloads it now, from scratch (staging files are per transport). If
none is left, the message is set to `Failure` and dequeued, which puts the
retry button back.

`download_msg()`'s "no location known on any transport" branch no longer
waits forever either. That state is either a post-message that has not
arrived yet or one that is gone, and nothing tells them apart, so it waits
while the pre-message is fresh — ten minutes, since the sender may still be
uploading a large attachment — and fails it after that. Inbox loops wake at
least every 5-minute IDLE timeout, so one that never comes fails within
about 15 minutes. A relay that proved the message gone fails it at once.

Webimap transports fetch every message in full and never serve the
`download` queue, so a download tapped there for a post-message that never
reaches the relay spun forever too. The webimap loop now runs the same
grace-then-fail check over the queue once per round.

The location lookup is now `imap_location()`, named because its
transport-spanning behavior is load-bearing: only a message no relay has a
location for may be failed. It ignores rows of transports that were removed
(they linger until housekeeping, up to a day), which would otherwise keep a
message "held by another relay" that no loop serves.

When the probe forgets a location it also deletes that location's staging
file, which can never be resumed and would otherwise sit in the blobdir
until housekeeping. And `download_full()` uses INSERT OR IGNORE, so tapping
download while an auto-download of the same message is queued no longer
returns an error to the UI. Tests cover the lookup across transports and
the forget decision with two relays, without involving the flag.

An empty FETCH is only taken as proof from a session that still completes
a tagged NOOP: async-imap ends the stream without saying so on a dropped
connection or a rejected command, and forgetting a location on either would
leave a retry with nothing to fetch from.

The probe runs before the chunked/whole-message split: `FLAGS
RFC822.SIZE` is plain RFC 3501, so servers without partial FETCH get the
same proof. Any other fetch error is not proof that the message is gone and
keeps the download queued, to retry on the next cycle.

`download_msgs()` now fails a message that is still `InProgress` after a
fetch reported `Done`: receive_imf rejecting or trashing it, or a FETCH
response without a body, dropped the queue entry while nothing moved the
message on. That covers the chunked and the whole-message path in one
place. `set_state_to_failure()` now only acts on `InProgress`, so a message
another transport finished meanwhile is never flipped back to `Failure`,
and one nobody asked for keeps its plain download button.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
